      "abstract": "\nFichte\u2019s distinction, in his Wissenschaftslehre of 1812, between what shifts from visibility into invisibility and vice versa \u2013 respectively, reality (A) and thought (B) \u2013 operates upon a chiastic premise. First, their difference can be described as a shift in the focus \u2013 which takes places at the B-level \u2013 from one to the other. In short, then, A and B stand in a relation of inverse proportionality. Secondly, their being brought together as each other\u2019s reverse (or multiplicative inverse) does not erase their difference \u2013 quite the opposite: it only serves to highlight it. Lastly, the fact that it is only from B\u2019s self-reflective perspective that B and then, ultimately, A become visible in their difference hints at the fact that their unity and their difference is purely thinkable. Therefore, against the dominant view (which I analyze in section 8) that Fichte gradually moved from a philosophy of the \u201cI\u201d to one of the \u201cabsolute\u201d of which the 1812 Wissenschaftslehre is often taken to be the final expression I contend (in sections 2\u20135) following Isabelle Thomas-Fogiel, that Fichte\u2019s ongoing enquiry into the problem of thought\u2019s self-transparency \u2013 which, I further argue, haunts the history of philosophy from Parmenides and Plato to Wittgenstein \u2013 reaches its zenith in this text. Concerning the dissymmetric correlation of being and thought in it, I moreover offer (in sections 6\u20137) both a structuralist interpretation \u2013 in dialogue with Cristiana Senigaglia and Max Marcuzzi\u2019s novel approaches to Fichte\u2019s late ontology \u2013 and a diagrammatic representation. Finally, after examining (in section 9) Heidegger\u2019s reliance on Fichte and the way in which the ambiguity of Heidegger\u2019s own thought becomes apparent in light of it (regardless of whether one focusses on the first or the second Heidegger), I assess (in section 10) the manner in which a structuralist reinterpretation of Fichte\u2019s philosophy might influence the contemporary discussion on the dissociation or, alternatively, the intertwining of earth and world. This would also reveal a chance to move beyond post-structuralism and into a neo-structuralist type of thinking which I explore in dialogue with Roy Wagner\u2019s meta-conceptual use of the notion of inverse proportionality.",
